1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,173
Add one drop of acid to pure
water, and its pH crashes.

2
00:00:04,173 --> 00:00:07,692
Add the same drop to blood,
and the pH barely moves.

3
00:00:08,092 --> 00:00:11,228
Why does blood fight
back, when water gives up?

4
00:00:11,678 --> 00:00:14,241
Aarav races his bicycle up a steep road.

5
00:00:14,241 --> 00:00:16,559
His muscles work hard and make acid.

6
00:00:16,909 --> 00:00:19,838
Yet his blood stays at
about pH seven point four.

7
00:00:19,838 --> 00:00:22,403
If it moved much, he would be in danger.

8
00:00:22,903 --> 00:00:26,870
By the end, you will know the trick,
and calculate the pH of the mixture

9
00:00:26,870 --> 00:00:27,772
that does it.

10
00:00:28,422 --> 00:00:30,396
Think of a football goal
with two goalkeepers.

11
00:00:30,746 --> 00:00:32,804
Keeper one catches every acid shot.

12
00:00:32,804 --> 00:00:34,862
Keeper two catches every base shot.

13
00:00:35,262 --> 00:00:39,835
In chemistry, keeper one is a weak
base part, and keeper two is a weak

14
00:00:39,835 --> 00:00:40,735
acid part.

15
00:00:40,690 --> 00:00:42,765
Both sit in the same solution.

16
00:00:43,165 --> 00:00:45,579
A solution with both keepers is a buffer.

17
00:00:45,579 --> 00:00:49,216
It resists a change in pH when
a little acid or base is added.

18
00:00:49,816 --> 00:00:52,261
Let's test it on our molecule bench.

19
00:00:52,611 --> 00:00:56,168
On the left, one litre
of pure water, pH seven.

20
00:00:56,168 --> 00:01:01,127
On the right, one litre of acetic acid
and sodium acetate, point one mole

21
00:01:01,127 --> 00:01:02,027
of each.

22
00:01:02,332 --> 00:01:06,253
Now add point zero one mole
of hydrochloric acid to both.

23
00:01:06,653 --> 00:01:08,575
In water, nothing catches the protons.

24
00:01:08,575 --> 00:01:10,284
The pH crashes from seven to two.

25
00:01:10,684 --> 00:01:14,622
In the buffer, acetate ions catch
the protons and become acetic acid.

26
00:01:14,622 --> 00:01:18,535
The pH only moves from four point
seven six to four point six seven.

27
00:01:19,135 --> 00:01:20,956
There are two kinds of buffers.

28
00:01:21,306 --> 00:01:25,709
An acidic buffer is a weak acid
with its salt of a strong base.

29
00:01:25,709 --> 00:01:30,350
Acetic acid with sodium acetate works
around pH four point seven five.

30
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:35,125
A basic buffer is a weak base
with its salt of a strong acid.

31
00:01:35,125 --> 00:01:39,679
Ammonium hydroxide with ammonium
chloride works around pH nine point two

32
00:01:39,679 --> 00:01:40,579
five.

33
00:01:40,921 --> 00:01:42,722
So how do we find a buffer's pH?

34
00:01:42,722 --> 00:01:43,622
Step by step.

35
00:01:43,926 --> 00:01:44,826
Step one.

36
00:01:44,725 --> 00:01:45,935
Start from K a.

37
00:01:45,935 --> 00:01:49,388
K a equals hydronium times
acetate, over acetic acid.

38
00:01:50,138 --> 00:01:51,038
Step two.

39
00:01:50,994 --> 00:01:51,894
Rearrange.

40
00:01:51,839 --> 00:01:55,107
Hydronium equals K a
times acid, over acetate.

41
00:01:55,857 --> 00:01:56,757
Step three.

42
00:01:56,729 --> 00:01:58,681
Take minus log of both sides.

43
00:01:59,431 --> 00:02:00,331
Step four.

44
00:02:00,245 --> 00:02:03,106
pH equals p K a plus
log of salt over acid.

45
00:02:03,106 --> 00:02:05,686
This is the Henderson
Hasselbalch equation.

46
00:02:06,436 --> 00:02:07,336
Step five.

47
00:02:07,335 --> 00:02:10,693
When salt and acid are
equal, the log is zero.

48
00:02:10,693 --> 00:02:12,304
So pH equals p K a.

49
00:02:13,054 --> 00:02:16,381
For a basic buffer, use
the same idea with K b.

50
00:02:16,731 --> 00:02:19,998
pOH equals p K b plus
log of salt over base.

51
00:02:19,998 --> 00:02:22,215
Then pH is fourteen minus pOH.

52
00:02:22,615 --> 00:02:23,781
Now, your blood.

53
00:02:23,781 --> 00:02:27,246
Its main buffer is carbonic acid
with hydrogen carbonate ions.

54
00:02:27,646 --> 00:02:30,222
If acid comes in, hydrogen
carbonate catches it.

55
00:02:30,222 --> 00:02:32,599
If base comes in,
carbonic acid catches it.

56
00:02:32,999 --> 00:02:37,496
And adding water to a buffer barely
changes its pH, because the ratio of

57
00:02:37,496 --> 00:02:39,465
salt to acid stays the same.

58
00:02:40,065 --> 00:02:41,526
Compare two buffers.

59
00:02:41,526 --> 00:02:43,885
Buffer one, equal acid and salt.

60
00:02:43,885 --> 00:02:46,725
Buffer two, twice as much salt as acid.

61
00:02:47,125 --> 00:02:48,054
Pause and think.

62
00:02:48,054 --> 00:02:49,685
Which buffer has the higher pH?

63
00:02:52,885 --> 00:02:53,785
Buffer two.

64
00:02:53,752 --> 00:02:58,145
More salt means the log term is
positive, so the pH is above p K a.

65
00:02:58,795 --> 00:02:59,839
Remember this.

66
00:02:59,839 --> 00:03:02,824
pH equals p K a plus
log of salt over acid.

67
00:03:03,524 --> 00:03:05,213
Now, one worked example.

68
00:03:05,513 --> 00:03:10,678
A buffer has point one molar acetic
acid and point two molar sodium acetate.

69
00:03:10,678 --> 00:03:13,033
p K a is four point seven six.

70
00:03:13,033 --> 00:03:14,121
Find its pH.

71
00:03:14,621 --> 00:03:17,851
pH equals p K a plus
log of salt over acid.

72
00:03:18,601 --> 00:03:21,801
Salt over acid is point
two over point one.

73
00:03:21,801 --> 00:03:22,922
That is two.

74
00:03:23,672 --> 00:03:26,200
Log of two is point three zero one.

75
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:29,777
So pH is four point seven
six plus point three zero.

76
00:03:29,777 --> 00:03:31,296
Five point zero six.

77
00:03:32,046 --> 00:03:35,423
More salt than acid, so
the pH is just above p K a.

78
00:03:36,073 --> 00:03:37,535
Now, the exam trap.

79
00:03:37,885 --> 00:03:41,197
Students flip the ratio and
write log of acid over salt.

80
00:03:41,597 --> 00:03:43,235
It is salt over acid.

81
00:03:43,235 --> 00:03:47,994
Flip it and the answer comes out four
point four six instead of five point

82
00:03:47,994 --> 00:03:48,894
zero six.

83
00:03:49,483 --> 00:03:50,943
Where is this asked?

84
00:03:50,943 --> 00:03:55,332
Boards ask what a buffer is, with
one acidic and one basic example.

85
00:03:55,332 --> 00:03:59,907
Entrance tests ask for the pH or the
ratio, using Henderson Hasselbalch.

86
00:04:00,507 --> 00:04:01,748
Exam level now.

87
00:04:01,748 --> 00:04:06,355
A buffer of ammonia and ammonium
chloride must have pH nine point seven

88
00:04:06,355 --> 00:04:08,679
five at two ninety eight kelvin.

89
00:04:08,679 --> 00:04:11,077
p K b is four point seven five.

90
00:04:11,077 --> 00:04:14,158
What ratio of ammonium
to ammonia is needed?

91
00:04:14,158 --> 00:04:15,451
Pause and solve.

92
00:04:20,851 --> 00:04:22,187
The answer is B.

93
00:04:22,187 --> 00:04:24,101
About point three one six.

94
00:04:24,601 --> 00:04:27,802
pOH equals fourteen minus
nine point seven five.

95
00:04:27,802 --> 00:04:29,326
Four point two five.

96
00:04:29,826 --> 00:04:34,618
Four point two five equals four point
seven five plus log of the ratio.

97
00:04:34,618 --> 00:04:37,500
So log of the ratio is minus point five.

98
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,571
Ratio is ten to the minus point five.

99
00:04:40,571 --> 00:04:42,415
About point three one six.

100
00:04:42,415 --> 00:04:43,900
A flipped the ratio.

101
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:46,040
Here is your recap.

102
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:47,623
Screenshot this card.

103
00:04:50,823 --> 00:04:53,009
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

104
00:04:53,409 --> 00:04:54,309
One.

105
00:04:53,840 --> 00:04:56,011
What two things make an acidic buffer?

106
00:04:56,811 --> 00:04:57,711
Two.

107
00:04:57,146 --> 00:04:59,549
Why does a buffer's pH barely
change when you add water?

108
00:05:00,349 --> 00:05:01,249
Three.

109
00:05:00,899 --> 00:05:04,490
If salt and acid are equal,
what is the pH of the buffer?

110
00:05:05,391 --> 00:05:09,516
Next time: sugar keeps dissolving
in water, but chalk hardly dissolves

111
00:05:09,516 --> 00:05:10,416
at all.

112
00:05:10,189 --> 00:05:11,089
Why?

113
00:05:10,667 --> 00:05:12,828
We find out in Solubility Product.
